# The maintainer's adjacency ruling on issue #944's sibling question: **a newline separates exactly
# like a space — always, everywhere.** Adjacency is whitespace-agnostic; a newline is insignificant
# to the *grammar*, not invisible to the *lexer*.
#
# This is a NARROWING. A newline token's `end` is the next line's column 1, so once a container
# skipped it the following `[` tested as adjacent — a skipped newline *manufactured* an adjacency
# the source never had, and `[ :nums` ⏎ `[1] ]` read `[1]` as a selector on `:nums` while
# `[ :nums [1] ]` read a second element. The two spellings now agree.
#
# A list literal is the container used here because both spellings are execution-valid in it, so
# the ruling can be proven as a VALUE rather than as a diagnostic. The malformed containers — a
# parenthesized group, and the top level, where the spaced spelling is an arity error for reasons
# unrelated to adjacency — have their agreement pinned in the companion check fixture instead.
#
# The glued spelling is printed too and must NOT match, or this fixture would still pass if
# adjacency stopped working altogether.
:nums = [ 10 20 30 ]

# Newline, then space: each reads `:nums` and then a separate one-element list.
print [ :nums
[1] ]
print [ :nums [1] ]

# Glued: `[1]` is a selector, so this prints the element rather than a second list.
print [ :nums[1] ]

# The regression this direction had to avoid. A newline after a comprehension header selects the
# long body form (`spec/grammar.md:34`), so the `[` binds as a BODY, not as a selector on `:nums`.
# A narrowing can only make the `[` less likely to bind, so this is strictly more protected than
# before — asserted here rather than assumed.
print map n in :nums [ :n ]
